 "drivingQuestion": "From where is it derived that idolatry rulings require partial nullification?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "the baraita's answer continues in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "horayot.7b.8",
   "daf": "7b",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "וְלֹא בַּעֲבוֹדָה זָרָה כּוּ׳. מְנָלַן? דְּתָנוּ רַבָּנַן: לְפִי שֶׁיָּצְאָה עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה לָדוּן בְּעַצְמָהּ, יָכוֹל יְהוּ חַיָּיבִין עַל עֲקִירַת מִצְוָה כּוּלָּהּ?",
   "en": "The mishna continues: «And» the court and the priest are «not» liable for a ruling «with regard to idol worship» unless they issue a ruling to nullify part of that mitzva and to sustain part of it. The Gemara asks: «From where do we» derive this? It is derived «as the Sages taught» in a baraita: «Due to» the fact «that idol worship left» the category of unwitting transgressions, «to be discussed by itself» (see Numbers, chapter 15), one «might» have thought «that» the court and the anointed priest «would be liable» even «for nullifying the mitzva in its entirety.»"
  }
 ],
